[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data Types in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"MongoDB supports a variety of data types, allowing you to store diverse information within your documents. Understanding these data types is crucial for efficient data modeling and querying."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Common MongoDB Data Types"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Double": Represents 64-bit floating-point numbers. Follows the IEEE 754 standard."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":""String": UTF-8 strings. MongoDB strings should be valid UTF-8."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Object": Embedded documents. Allows you to nest documents within documents."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Array": An ordered list of values. Can contain elements of different data types."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Binary data": Binary data can be stored. Max size is 16 MB."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":""ObjectId": A 12-byte BSON type. Provides a unique identifier for documents."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Boolean": Represents true or false values."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Date": Stores the current date or time as the number of milliseconds since the Unix epoch (Jan 1, 1970)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Null": Represents a missing or undefined value."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Regular Expression": Stores regular expressions for pattern matching."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":""JavaScript code": JavaScript code can be stored and executed on the server."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Symbol": Used for language-specific symbols."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Int": Represents 32-bit integers."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Timestamp": Represents a 64-bit value where the first 32 bits are a seconds value (like a Unix timestamp), and the second 32 bits are an incrementing ordinal for operations within a given second."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Long": Represents 64-bit integers."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":""Decimal": Represents 128-bit decimal-based floating point numbers. Can represent numbers with very high precision."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":""MinKey": Compares as less than all other values."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":""MaxKey": Compares as greater than all other values."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Example Document"},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"{ 
  "_id": ObjectId("64f1a7b3e4b0a7b3e4b0a7b3"),
  "name": "John Doe",
  "age": 30,
  "isStudent": false,
  "address": {
    "street": "123 Main St",
    "city": "Anytown"
  },
  "grades": [90, 85, 92]
}"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, '_id' is an ObjectId, 'name' is a String, 'age' is an Int, 'isStudent' is a Boolean, 'address' is an Object (embedded document), and 'grades' is an Array."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Data Type Considerations"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the appropriate data type is important for several reasons:"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Storage Efficiency: Using the correct data type can minimize storage space."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Query Performance: Data types affect how MongoDB indexes and queries data."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Integrity: Using the correct data type helps ensure data consistency and validity."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Type Conversion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"MongoDB performs implicit type conversion in some cases. However, it's generally best practice to ensure that your data is stored in the correct type to avoid unexpected behavior. You can use aggregation pipelines to explicitly convert data types if needed."}]
